{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/panel-count-encoding.md", "content": "# Panel marker-count encoding: diagnostic and validation lesson

This instance had grids partitioned by full separator bands and compact outputs shaped like the panel array. Several demos were consistent with an output marker for panels containing multiple instances of the designated non-background color; others had different marker colors and output backgrounds. A test answer based on a guessed threshold and manually counted panels was rejected, so the evidence does not establish the exact test encoding or make a reusable color/threshold rule.

Reusable method: transcribe the grid exactly; locate separator rows/columns; define half-open panel bounds; count target-color cells in every panel; build a table of panel coordinates, counts, and corresponding output values. Test competing count mappings (including exact count versus threshold) against *all* demonstrations, especially panels with zero, one, two, and three-or-more markers. Confirm orientation, output dimensions, background encoding, and values separately. After rejection, first independently re-check serialization and the panel count table; do not repeat a merely reformatted candidate. Do not claim a count rule until full demo replay supports it.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen-2/play/users/u043/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-delta-discipline/references/panel-count-encoding.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/template-stamp-marker-separation.md", "content": "# Separating markers from a stamp template

For a grid with scattered single-color marks and a distinctive multicolor shape, map the output delta against the input before deciding the shape's role. Record each copied footprint's exact coordinates and colors, then compare those to both scattered markers and any instance embedded in the source template. The template's own center may or may not be an active marker; infer this from the demo, rather than assuming all similar-colored cells are equivalent.

Define one explicit anchor and offsets for every template cell, including vertical/asymmetric parts. Enumerate marker positions from the original input before applying any edits. Replay on each demo and compare the full output, including unchanged cells, overlap, clipping, and whether the source template persists. A visually plausible generic cross is not a substitute for the observed exact template.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen-2/play/users/u043/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-delta-discipline/references/template-stamp-marker-separation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/compact-output-rejection-lesson.md", "content": "# Compact-output rejection: reassess representation

A trial on a partitioned grid with 6×4 cells separated by full-width divider rows produced a 3×3 demo output. The initial response incorrectly submitted a speculative 5×5 grid, effectively disregarding the demonstrated output scale and region structure. The next action requested another demo without articulating a discriminating prediction. On future tasks, an output whose dimensions are smaller than the input should immediately trigger compact region mapping: locate separators, count regions, associate regions to output locations, and test marker presence/color/count/position against all observed outputs. After an incorrect submission, reopen task class and output representation before trying again. Request further demos only after naming competing mappings and the specific observation that will distinguish them.

Note: the currently available demo in this case shows 11×14 input and 3×3 output, so infer only a 3×3 relation from it; do not assume a 6×3 output or transfer an unrelated example. The test input must be evaluated under the demonstrated output shape and its own divider structure.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen-2/play/users/u043/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-delta-discipline/references/compact-output-rejection-lesson.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-incorrect-submission-recovery", "file": "references/continual-turn-lessons.md", "content": "# Continual ARC turn completeness and evidence boundaries

In continual puzzle interfaces, each message—including incorrect feedback—requires one legal action JSON on the final line. A thoughtful diagnosis without a valid action leaves the turn incomplete.

After a rejection, use references from previously encountered sessions only as process cautions. They are not evidence that this task shares the referenced transformation; do not transplant specific rules or guessed output grids. Reconstruct the current examples' deltas and representations, and demand exact replay of every available demo before another submission. If no supported candidate exists, request a demo only when it answers a concrete ambiguity that could change the output.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen-2/play/users/u043/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-incorrect-submission-recovery/references/continual-turn-lessons.md"}
